00:00:00.080 --> 00:00:02.310 align:start position:0%
 
hi my name is santos lowe i'm an la  based food stylist and today i'm going  to show you how i style a barbecue bacon  cheeseburger with creamy slaw  when i'm thinking about this burger i'm  thinking about the bottom to the top and  i'm also thinking about the angle of the  camera in this case we're going to shoot  the burger at eye level when i build  this burger i've run it through my head  not exaggerating  probably 300 times for this burger i'm  gonna use a brioche bun  i love using brioche bun because it's  already glamorous all i need to do is  give it a nice toast making sure that  it's golden i like to use a cast iron  skillet because it gives me more even  heat so i'm not gonna have parts of the  bun that are more toasted than the other  part  once the bun is down i rotate the bun  just to ensure that i get even toasting  i constantly check the bun and i like to  press the edges of the bun as a toast  just to make sure that it's making  contact with the pan  once the bottom bun is nicely toasted on  the inside  roll it around just a little bit on the  edges so i'm not looking for necessarily  an even browning just a little bit here  and there i think that'll give it a nice  touch in the end  now it's time for the bacon  i chose a thick cut maple bacon because  ultimately this burger is going to be  pretty stacked and i want you to be able  to see the bacon immediately start the  pan on a medium-low heat if the pan were  on too high heat the bacon would just  fry and overcook quickly  when cooking this bacon i like not to  put it on the pan flat nestle it in the  pan in a way so that it curls when i put  it on the burger these curves are going  to give the burger volume and it's also  going to create shelves for the slaw to  hang onto and rest on as i'm turning the  bacon over i'm allowing all these  natural curls to happen helping it  happen by lifting it if i have to  once the bacon is done i'm just going to  set it aside and get ready to cook the  patties  lightly coat the cast iron pan with a  little bit of vegetable oil too much oil  is going to fry my meat  a blend of 80 20 which means 80 percent  lean beef to 20 percent fat this blend  is going to yield a patty that has more  texture i like to use my fingers to  press the meat from the center to the  outside using my thumb and my finger it  doesn't necessarily have to be perfect  but it should be round any cracks that i  see right now i want to make sure to  pinch those together because as it cooks  the crack will just split more form the  patty so that it is about a half inch  bigger than the the bun itself it's  going to shrink a little bit but not  very much the cooked patty should stick  out a quarter inch or so i like to press  the edges of the patty to make sure that  i get contact my approach is to first  sear it on a flat cast iron scale i'm  then going to transfer it onto a grill  pan and give it those nice grill marks  and then just carefully and gently  sear the edges  the edges are important because that's  what you're going to see  now i'm heating my cast iron grill pan a  meat that is fried is going to look too  crusty a meat that is seared is going to  yield a deeper brown  color  grease the ridges just a little bit with  vegetable oil and once the pan starts to  smoke i'm going to take my burger patty  and i'm going to press it into the marks  firm but gentle i don't want to  move this around too much because i want  the marks to be clear  and bold see these little crevices in  here that's really nice all right these  burger patties are looking really good  right now  later if i have to cut this in half i  know that it's going to be a juicy  medium well done burger  i have the standard slaw ingredients  cabbage carrot and some green onions now  for the cabbage i've selected green and  purple just to add that touch of color  whenever i'm working with cabbage i like  to peel away the first layer or two to  reveal the more fresher crispier  interior  when it comes time to  slice cabbage for a slaw the lengthwise  sliced cabbage yields a better product  for this recipe it's lighter and as you  can see here it's gonna provide me a lot  of volume when preparing anything for a  food styling job i'm always thinking two  or three steps ahead so i know that this  creamy dressing that i'm gonna fold into  this salad is gonna weigh the vegetables  down and so if i can cut the cabbage in  a way which gives me volume it's going  to help prevent that when cooking food i  get into a zone and i begin to think  several steps ahead because this is  gonna pay off later the next vegetable  i'm gonna prepare is the carrots i'm  just gonna simply cut these into  two to three inch  long segments and then thinly slice  these into planks stack the planks  together i want to cut these super thin  so that they give me volume  and here we go with the scallion i'm  going to cut these as thinly as i can  just as i did the carrots and the  cabbage the end product is going to look  so so good  all my veggies are prepped i'm going to  set these aside and prepare the coleslaw  dressing  for this dressing i have the usual  suspects i have of course the mayo i'm  gonna add a little bit of yellow mustard  for tang i love using smoked paprika in  this because it's going to give the  overall dressing a  nice rose color  let's add a couple dashes of vinegar  and i also like to add honey sometimes  recipes like this call for sugar but i  instead like to use honey because it'll  also give the dressing a thickness  let's look at how the dressing clings to  the whisk imagine the whisk being pieces  of cabbage  this is looking really good it's coating  the whisk  and it's dripping but it's not running  off of it  now that we have all of our prep done  it's finally time to build this bacon  barbecue cheeseburger  flavor-wise  this burger's gonna be fantastic but my  priority is  is a visual one the first thing that i  ask is how is the food being shot in  this case the burger is being shot at  eye level i am designing this burger to  that angle 70 of it is probably game  plan and 30  i'm just gonna leave up to the moment um  i'm gonna improvise it's what's gonna  make this burger special let's give this  bun a little bit of shine we gave it  color now it's time to highlight that  color and i'm simply going to do this by  touching it here and there with a little  bit of vegetable oil not too much i  don't want to grease this bun down but i  definitely want to highlight some of  those beautiful toasted areas i picked a  barbecue sauce that was on the red side  as opposed to a brown barbecue sauce  when the burger patty is placed on top  the sauce is going to gently fall over  the edge of the bun i'm also going to  add a little bit of sauce underneath one  side of the bun just to make it look  like the sauce fell over the bun i just  think it looks really nice  a burger like this has to have pickles  these are what's called bread and butter  pickles want to pick out the ones that  are not perfectly round but that have a  more organic shape to them and i like to  pull them off so that they  hang over the bun just a little bit and  i love the green color of these pickles  too i noticed that the the shelf stable  pickles are not as bright as these  here's the burger patty that we seared  with the really nice grill marks i'm  just going to brush a little bit of oil  onto sides and around the edges  all right here's the cheddar cheese how  i place this cheese is very important  because once i melt the cheese the  cheese is stuck to the meat and there  are many ways to melt cheese on a burger  patty when food styling i like to use a  heat gun because i feel it just gives me  more control i want to make sure to melt  the hard edges of the cheese so that  they become more round and begin to hug  that meat just a little bit now the  cheese is melted on the patty i have to  make sure that i put this exposed part  of the burger facing the front of the  camera the burger patty is the star of  the show oh that looks really good  the next part of this is the bacon the  bacon just like the beef patty just  needs a little bit of love brushing it  with oil makes it look like it was just  cooked  the next thing that goes on now is this  law i'm simply just going to  dollop some of this dressing directly  onto the vegetables and mix them with my  hand i feel that this is going to give  me more control and then what i like to  do is just gently massage the sauce into  the slaw and then build a mound that i  like before i add the slaw i need to  prep the top bun and so just like i did  with the bottom bun  i am going to add a little bit of oil to  some of the toasted areas around the  edge and then i'm going to spread some  barbecue sauce on it as well  the one thing that i know about a bacon  barbecue cheeseburger is that it's  supposed to be a little bit messy and so  that is part of my goal is to have a  little bit of drippage a little bit of  sauce on the plate like that's all good  just like in the arcade rooms where the  crane comes over and tries to grab that  toy i'm gonna gently grab a handful of  this law  carefully place it onto the burger  and now all i need to do is just give  some of these stray pieces of cabbage  some direction  okay now it's time to add the top of the  bun now once this is down it's down  there's no turning back from here here  we go  oh that feels really good  so as i'm putting this bun down i don't  want to smash all of that volume that i  created in the slaw i want to just sort  of nestle this sauce into the slaw  because this burger is all about height  i've allowed the food to speak if the  sauce wants to come over the bun and  spill onto the plate  that's great because that's what sauce  does if the cheese wants to melt a  little bit too far down the meat that's  great too because that's what cheese  does and so i feel that my job as a food  stylist is to help the food do what it  wants to do whenever i finish styling  food the first thing that comes to mind  is  how is the audience going to read this  is it going to be i want to eat this for  me  what makes a successful styled food that  needs to define the recipe i need to  look at it and immediately be able to  say that looks like what i was supposed  to make there's a feeling that i can't  really describe this feeling has to do  with subconsciously comparing it to  millions of images i've seen a burger  thousands of burgers that i've eaten and  hundreds of burgers that i've actually  styled and all of that just sort of  comes together as i'm staring at this  burger that i just made when i look at  it i can just know  it's good  or it's not  oh yes  [Music]  you

